A var handler's parameter is the value the var is being set to, and every
backend already holds that value: it is the setter's own parameter. So the
handler body only has to agree on the spelling, and the parameter is bound by
renaming its references onto the setter's -- `v` on all three of these.

Before the rename the body was spliced verbatim, so the declared name reached
the output free: `undefined: nv` on bubbletea and fyne, and on html a
`state.count = nv + 1` that no error reports and no browser can run.

The parameter is deliberately not called `v`, since that is the one name the
old splice would have got right by accident.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `\bnv\b` -- the declared name is bound, never emitted
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `\bnv\b` -- same
deny out/none/html/index.html `\bnv\b` -- same, and here nothing would have reported it

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var count = 0

    var tracked = 0 @change(nv int) {
        count = nv + 1
    }

    vbox {
        button(text="+", @click {
            tracked = tracked + 1
        })
        text(value="{count}")
    }
}

window {
    main
}
